Over the 9,459 returning requests all five arms sent before minute 110 (HiCache, JIT p50, JIT exact, All four rules, Oracle): mean
TTFT 31.68, 5.60, 5.07, 5.09 and 4.85 s. Requests served in 2 h: 10,479, 17,049, 17,114, 17,095 and 17,206; users admitted: 469,
678, 677, 676 and 680 of the 729 that arrive.
Recorded waits at x1, one run per arm. 0.1 users arrive a second and at most 128 are in session; the others wait for a slot, a wait
TTFT leaves out. Prompts are synthetic token ids of the recorded lengths and outputs have the recorded length; sessions with a
request over 65,536 tokens are left out. Exact hints are the true next-request times; p50 hints are the median wait after the tool
call the reply makes, from a table the client keeps online for each harness and model. The rules arm gets that table's p10, p50 and
p90 and an end-of-session signal: it starts reads by expected cost at a learned price on host memory, evicts by chance of return,
and lets an arrived request's read take room from early reads. The oracle is the rules arm given each true next-request time as its
distribution, so its reads land as the request is due and it evicts the session that returns last first. Attention runs on
FlashAttention 3. Every arm runs the engine with the eviction fix: a request queued for over 60 s is no longer taken for one not
coming. Engine fixes on: pages the SSD already holds are not written again; queued writes are merged into transfers of up to 128
pages; timed reads wait while pending writes hold 50,000 host tokens; a read refused host memory is retried without spending the
turn's lookups.
